---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const SixRows = `WHAT YOU SUBMIT                              earns 2 points

  Video ............................. 0-1
  Program Requirements .............. 0-1

WHAT YOU WRITE ON EXAM DAY                   earns 4 points

  Written Response 1 ................ 0-1
  Written Response 2(a) ............. 0-1
  Written Response 2(b) ............. 0-1
  Written Response 2(c) ............. 0-1

                                     TOTAL   6`;

const FourPrompts = `WR1     Name an expected user of your program.
        Describe one way your design meets that user's needs.

WR2(a)  Find the FIRST loop in your Procedure segment.
        How many times does its body run?
        Describe a condition or error that would make it loop forever.

WR2(b)  Find the procedure in your Procedure (i) segment.
        Describe a change that would cause a RUN-TIME error,
        and explain why.

WR2(c)  You are given isEqual(value1, value2).
        Using YOUR list, explain in detailed steps an algorithm
        that counts how many elements equal a certain value.`;

const RequirementsList = `1  INPUT       the program asks for something and uses the answer

2  LIST        a list the program actually needs

3  PROCEDURE   one you wrote, with a parameter that changes
               what it does

4  ALGORITHM   sequencing, selection and iteration
               inside that procedure's body

5  CALLS       you call that procedure

6  OUTPUT      based on the input and the functionality`;

const NotStudentDeveloped = `function setup() { ... }          p5 calls it       NOT yours
function draw() { ... }           p5 calls it       NOT yours
function mousePressed() { ... }   p5 calls it       NOT yours
function keyPressed() { ... }     p5 calls it       NOT yours

function drawTree(x, y, size) { ... }               YOURS`;

const Attribution = `// This code was provided as starter code by my teacher.
function shuffle(list) {
  ...
}`;

const AuditGrid = `Open your Build 2 program. Answer from the code, not from memory.

  A list, more than one element ............ yes / no
  The list is actually used ................ yes / no
  A procedure you wrote .................... yes / no
  You call it .............................. yes / no
  Selection somewhere ...................... yes / no
  Iteration somewhere ...................... yes / no

  ALL SIX?  -> that row scores 1 on the real thing.`;

const PprCheck = `Find these four things in your Build 2 program.
Write the line number of each.

  Procedure (i)   the procedure, with its parameter        line ____
  Procedure (ii)  where you call it                        line ____
  List (i)        where data goes INTO the list            line ____
  List (ii)       where that list's data gets USED         line ____

Now cover your screen and answer WR2(a) about it.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.1: The Create Task &mdash; What It Is and What the Rules Are</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Where You Are</h2>

          <p>
            <b>The Create Performance Task is 30% of your AP exam grade.</b> You have
            twelve sessions: today and one more to prepare, then ten to build and
            submit.
          </p>

          <p>
            You have done this twice already. <b>Build 1 taught scoping. Build 2
            taught duration.</b> The requirements are the same six you have met
            before. <b class="text-sn-theme">What is different is that this one
            counts, and that from session 55 onward I cannot help you.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            Today is not a build session. <b>Today is the rules</b>, and one of them
            costs you the whole task if you get it wrong.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.1</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">The Create Task &mdash; What It Is and What the Rules Are</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Six Points, and Where They Come From</p>
              <p>What Counts as Yours</p>
              <p>The Rules That Cost Everything</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>It Is Scored Out of Six</h2>
              <Code code={SixRows} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Whole Rubric" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Six rows. Each one is worth exactly one point.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read That Again</h2>
              <p><b>Two points come from what you submit.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">Four come from what you write on exam day.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">About your own program. From memory. In sixty minutes.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So What Is the Program For?</h2>
              <p>It is worth two points directly.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And it is the <b>subject of the other four</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A program you cannot explain is a program that scores 2 out of 6,
                no matter how good it is.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Four Questions</h2>
              <Code code={FourPrompts} frame="code" lang="txt" title="You Will Answer These in May" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">You will have your Project Reference in front of you. Nothing else.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Have Practiced Three of Them</h2>
              <p><b>WR2(a)</b> is a trace. You have done twenty.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>WR2(b)</b> is a debug challenge in reverse.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>WR2(c)</b> is prose pseudocode &mdash; session 4, and every set since.</p>
              <p class="fragment">This is why those pages existed.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Six Requirements</h2>
              <Code code={RequirementsList} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Same Six as Build 2" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Nothing new. You have satisfied these twice.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What Counts as Yours</h2>
              <Code code={NotStudentDeveloped} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Student-Developed" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                The College Board: <i>event handlers are built-in abstractions and
                will therefore not be considered student-developed.</i>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>A Name for That</h2>
              <p>Code that runs when something happens, rather than in order from the top.</p>
              <p class="fragment">That is <b class="text-sn-theme">event-driven programming</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It is a real and useful way to build. <b>It is also not what
                requirement 3 is asking for.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Cannot Submit Build 2</h2>
              <p>The College Board: you may not submit work from <i>practice performance tasks</i>.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Build 1 and Build 2 were practice performance tasks.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Start something new. Today you are going to audit Build 2, not reuse it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Ways to Score Zero</h2>
              <p><b>1.</b> Comments left in the Project Reference.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>2.</b> Code you did not write, uncredited.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Not zero on that row. <b class="text-sn-theme">Zero on the whole
                task, including the four written responses.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>On AI</h2>
              <p><b>Not in this course.</b> You write your own code.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                The College Board allows it with credit. <b>This class does not
                allow it at all.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                And here is the part that is not about rules: <b>four of the six
                points are you explaining your own loop, from memory, in May.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Crediting What Is Not Yours</h2>
              <Code code={Attribution} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="How to Cite" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Starter code, a library, anything from a site. Name where it came from.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And <b>existing code must be extended</b> &mdash; added to, not handed in.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You May Work Together</h2>
              <p>On <b>the program code</b> &mdash; ideas, building, debugging, testing.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Not</b> on the video. <b class="text-sn-theme">Not</b> on the
                Project Reference.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Those two are yours alone, and that is a College Board rule.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Collaboration Is In the Rules at All</h2>
              <p>Because it produces better software, and the exam says so.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A program built by people with <b>different backgrounds and
                different blind spots</b> has fewer of them.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It is also <b>how bias gets caught</b> &mdash; which comes back at
                session 72.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Working Together Is a Skill</h2>
              <p>Communication.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Consensus building.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Conflict resolution.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Negotiation.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Named on the exam. Practiced in pairs.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Sessions 53 and 54</h2>
              <p>Preparation. <b>Ask me anything.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Ideas, scope, whether your plan is any good. This is the window.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Sessions 55 to 64</h2>
              <p>The official task. <b class="text-sn-theme">I cannot give you feedback.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Not on your code, not on your plan, not on your writing.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                I can fix a broken machine and I can show you how to make a PDF.{" "}
                <b>That is the whole list.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>So Use Tomorrow</h2>
              <p>Session 54 is the last day I can tell you your plan is too big.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Every question you save for session 57 is a question nobody answers.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Naming the six rubric rows and where each point comes from</li>
                <li>Explaining why four of six points depend on explaining your own code</li>
                <li>Identifying which procedures in a program are student-developed</li>
                <li>Stating the collaboration boundaries and the attribution requirement</li>
                <li>Auditing an existing program against the six requirements</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Student-developed</dt>
                  <dd>Written by you, individually or with a partner. Calls to libraries and event handlers do not count.</dd>
                  <dt>Event-driven programming</dt>
                  <dd>Program statements run when triggered, rather than through the sequential flow of control.</dd>
                  <dt>Personalized Project Reference</dt>
                  <dd>Four code segments you take into the exam. Comments stripped.</dd>
                  <dt>Attribution</dt>
                  <dd>Naming the origin of code you did not write. Required, and cheap.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- The rubric in detail -->
        <section id="rubric">
          <h2>The Six Rows, In Detail</h2>

          <p>
            <b>This is the whole rubric.</b> There is no partial credit inside a row
            &mdash; each one is one point or nothing.
          </p>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm align-middle">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Row</th>
                  <th>Earns the point when&hellip;</th>
                  <th class="text-end">Pts</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Video</b></td>
                  <td>The video shows the program <b>running</b>, with input, functionality, and output. Screenshots do not count.</td>
                  <td class="text-end">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Program Requirements</b></td>
                  <td>The code has a list, a procedure, a call to it, selection, and iteration.</td>
                  <td class="text-end">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Written Response 1</b></td>
                  <td>You name an expected user <b>and</b> describe one way the design meets that user's needs.</td>
                  <td class="text-end">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Written Response 2(a)</b></td>
                  <td>You state how many times the loop body runs <b>and</b> describe what would make it loop forever.</td>
                  <td class="text-end">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Written Response 2(b)</b></td>
                  <td>You describe a change causing a run-time error <b>and</b> explain why.</td>
                  <td class="text-end">1</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td><b>Written Response 2(c)</b></td>
                  <td>You explain an algorithm counting matching list elements, in enough detail that someone else could code it.</td>
                  <td class="text-end">1</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h5 class="mt-4">Three things that lose the Program Requirements point</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>The list has <b>one element</b>.</li>
            <li>The list is <b>never used</b>, or its use does not matter.</li>
            <li>The selection or the iteration is <b>trivial</b> &mdash; it does not change what the program does.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>Notice the word <i>and</i> in four of those rows.</b> Half an answer
            earns nothing. Written Response 1 with a user named and no design
            description is worth the same as a blank page.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Requirement 4 -->
        <section id="requirement-four">
          <h2>Requirement 4, One More Time</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Sequencing, selection and iteration inside your procedure's body.</b>{" "}
            You have been audited on this once already, at Build 2 session 49.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is something honest about it. <b>The Program Requirements row does
            not actually check that they are inside the procedure.</b> It only checks
            that they exist somewhere in the program.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Requirement 4 still decides two of your six
            points, and here is how.</b> Written Response 2(a) asks about the{" "}
            <b>first loop in your Procedure segment</b>. Written Response 2(b) asks
            about <b>the procedure in your Procedure (i) segment</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>If your procedure has no loop in it, there is nothing for you to
            answer.</b> Not a lost project point &mdash; a lost exam point, on a
            question you cannot skip.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <p>
            <b>No new code today.</b> Open your Build 2 program and be honest with
            it.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Audit Build 2 Against the Rubric</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Answer all six from the code in front of you, not from what you remember writing.</li>
            <li>Write the line number next to each <b>yes</b>.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={AuditGrid} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Program Requirements Row" class="mt-3" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>A <i>no</i> here is good news.</b> You found it in August rather than
            in May, on a program that was never going to be submitted anyway.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Find the Four Segments</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>These are the four things your Project Reference will hold.</li>
            <li>Find them in Build 2 and write down where they are.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={PprCheck} frame="code" lang="txt" title="The Four Segments" class="mt-3" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Then do the last line.</b> Cover the screen and answer Written
            Response 2(a) out loud to the person next to you: how many times does
            that loop run, and what would make it run forever?
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">That is the exam.</b> If it was hard with the
            program two feet away, it will be harder in May with only four code
            segments on the desk.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: One Idea, Written Down</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><b>Something new.</b> Not Build 2, and not Build 2 with features added.</li>
            <li>Two sentences on what it does, and one line naming what its list would hold.</li>
            <li>You will bring this to session 54 and turn it into a plan.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>This is not a commitment.</b> You can change it tomorrow. It exists so
            that session 54 does not open with a blank page &mdash; which is how
            students lose the one session where I am still allowed to help.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Before you leave, you should be able to answer these without looking.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>How many points is the Create Task worth, and how many come from what you submit?</li>
            <li>Name one procedure in your Build 2 program that is <b>not</b> student-developed.</li>
            <li>What are the two ways to score zero on the entire task?</li>
            <li>Which two components may you <b>not</b> collaborate on?</li>
            <li>Which sessions are the last ones where you can ask me whether your plan is any good?</li>
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>The last one matters most today.</b> The answer is <b>this session and
            the next</b>, and after that the honest answer to <i>is this good
            enough</i> is that I am not allowed to tell you.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Four of the six points are earned by explaining your own code from memory. Describe one thing that changes about how you will build, knowing that.</li>
            <li>Your Build 2 program failed at least one line of the Task 1 audit, or it passed all six. Either way &mdash; what will you do differently in the new program?</li>
            <li>The rules say you may collaborate on code but not on the video or the Project Reference. Why do you think those two are treated differently?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your completed audit, your four segment line numbers, and your one idea
            with its list.
          </p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
